==English==
{{was wotd|2011|June|26}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɒkə/}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|ocker|lang=enm}}, {{term|oker|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|non}} {{term|ókr||usury|lang=non}}, from {{proto|Germanic|wōkraz|progeny, earnings, profit|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|aweg-||wōg-||aug-|to add, increase|lang=en}}. More at {{l|en|oker}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|Now|_|chiefly|_|dialectal}} [[interest|Interest]] on [[money]]; [[usury]]; [[increase]].

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{transitive|Now|_|chiefly|_|dialectal}} To [[increase]] (in price); [[add]] to.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|ockerer}}
* {{l|en|ockering}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{term|Ocker}}, pet form of the name {{term|Oscar}}; popularised in a series of television sketches where the word was used as a general nickname.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{slang|Australia}} A boorish or uncultivated Australian.
#* '''2011''', Ronald Bergan, ''The Guardian'', 23 May 2011:
#*: For many Australians, the screen persona of the character actor Bill Hunter, who has died of cancer aged 71, was the archetypal "'''ocker'''", an uncultivated Australian working man who enjoys beer, "barbies", Aussie rules football and V8 supercars.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj}}

# Pertaining to an ocker.
#* '''1992''', Will Self, ''Cock and Bull'':
#*:‘Non-erotic male bonding, that’s the thing isn’t it; what our '''ocker''' cousins call “mateyness”.’
#* '''2011''', Emily Portell, ''Herald Sun'' (Melbourne), 25 Jan 2011:
#*: Melbourne surf shop Mordy Surf triggered outrage after posting the YouTube clip, in which an '''ocker''' man says he is "gonna get a glass and smash it on some poof", on its website.

